// The revl stdlib JSON module (FR-3, docs/stdlib-json.md).
//
// Real tool calls carry structured args (DSH tools take JSON); before this
// module the harness's mock wire protocol had to be flattened to
// `TOOL_CALL name arg1 arg2` and parsed by hand. These two externs give a
// component the JSON story the harness needs, scoped to the value types
// revl already carries (Str/Int/Bool/Float/List/Opt/records): `parse` turns
// a Str into a revl value (`Any` — the wildcard the type algebra uses for
// values whose static type only the runtime knows), `stringify` turns one
// back into a Str.
//
// Each tier's implementation is the `@<backend>` body beside the signature
// (the extern-body mechanism, docs/syntax-2.0.md §extern). Import with a
// named `use`:
//
//     use "stdlib/json.rvl" { json_parse, json_stringify }
//
// Tier status (see docs/stdlib-json.md for the full table and the path to
// the remaining tiers):
//   * py — `json.loads` / `json.dumps`; executed by the test suite.
//   * ts — `JSON.parse` / `JSON.stringify`; emitted by the test suite.
//   * rs — an `Any` extern return type-erases to `cordis::Value` on this
//     tier, so a parsed record cannot be read back (no field access); the
//     module ships no @rs body until the emitter types Any-typed bindings
//     by their declared type. Refused with the honest "no @rs body" message.
//   * java — where a provider's Jackson/Gson plugs in; no JDK JSON API, so
//     none until a provider is on the `javac` classpath.
//   * go — needs the emitter to add `encoding/json` to the module's
//     imports for verbatim extern bodies (not yet wired); same refusal.
//   * wasm — the substrate value model (Int/Bool/Str/List) cannot hold
//     Float/Map/records, so a JSON value has no representation there.
//
// The `Any` return is what makes `parse` usable: it flows into any typed
// position (`let tc: ToolCall = json_parse(s)`), which is how a harness
// recovers a record of tool args from a wire string.

pub extern pure fn json_parse(s: Str) -> Any
  = @py { import json; return json.loads(s) }
  = @ts { return JSON.parse(s) }

pub extern pure fn json_stringify(v: Any) -> Str
  = @py { import json; return json.dumps(v) }
  = @ts { return JSON.stringify(v) }
